  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T06:00:36.114Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nIn addition to filing the annual income tax return, taxpayers must submit supporting documents such as financial statements, proof of income, and any other relevant documents as required by the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR).\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThe primary authority governing the requirements for filing an income tax return is found in Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which outlines the requirements for individual returns.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51 of the NIRC specifies that individuals are required to file an income tax return and must include necessary documentation to substantiate their reported income and deductions. This typically includes financial statements, proof of income (such as certificates of withholding tax), and any other documents that may be required by the BIR to verify the accuracy of the return.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nTaxpayers should ensure they have all necessary documentation ready when filing their annual income tax return to avoid delays or issues with the BIR. Proper documentation not only supports the accuracy of the return but also helps in case of audits or inquiries from the tax authorities.",
